# Curating annotations

Curators review the annotations submitted by all annotators for an article and
produce a single, authoritative annotation.

## Opening the curation view

Navigate to **Curation Queue** in the sidebar. Each article shows how many
annotators have submitted their work. Click an article to open the curation view.

## Layout

The curation view is split into two panels:

- **Left** — the decision tables where you accept or reject entities, mentions, and relations.
- **Right** — the article text (abstract and full text) with all annotated spans highlighted. Clicking a highlight focuses the corresponding mention in the left panel, and vice versa.

## Reviewing entities and mentions

The **Entities** table lists every entity that at least one annotator tagged.
Click a row to expand it and see that entity's individual mentions grouped by
the surface text they match.

Each mention shows a short excerpt of surrounding context and a badge indicating
whether it appears in the abstract or the full text.

![As a curator, you can review proposed entities and mentions](/screenshots/entity_review.png)

**Accepting and rejecting** works at three levels:

- **Entity row** — the `✓` button on the left accepts or removes the entity and
  all its mentions at once.
- **Mention group header** — the `✓` button next to a surface-text group accepts
  or rejects every occurrence of that exact phrasing.
- **Individual mention** — the `×` / `✓` button on a single mention item overrides
  it independently.

By default, all mentions are accepted. Start from the entity level and drill down
only where you need finer control.

### Agreement colours

Row backgrounds give a quick signal of annotator agreement:

- **Green** — all annotators tagged this entity or mention.
- **Yellow** — a majority did.
- **Red** — only a minority did.
- **Teal** — this entry was accepted in a previously saved curation for this article.

## Proposed entities

Entities not yet part of the project's knowledge graph are marked **proposed**.
Before saving, each proposed entity needs a confirmed identifier (CURIE):

1. Click the identifier shown in the **Identifier** column to open an inline editor.
2. Type the correct CURIE and press **Enter** to save, or **Escape** to cancel.
3. Once confirmed, a `✓` badge replaces the `?` badge next to the identifier.

If you want to keep the proposed identifier as-is, click the `?` badge and press
**Enter** without changing the text — this marks it as reviewed.

## Keyboard shortcuts

| Key | Action |
|---|---|
| `↓` or `j` | Move to the next mention |
| `↑` or `k` | Move to the previous mention |
| `Space` | Accept or reject the focused mention |

## Relations

If annotators have recorded relations between entities they appear in a
**Relations** table below the entity list. Use the `✓` button to include or
exclude each relation from the curated annotation.

## Saving and completing

Changes are **saved automatically** a moment after each edit — no manual save
needed. A status indicator in the bottom-left of the decision panel confirms
when the last save completed.

When you are satisfied with all decisions, click **Mark as complete**. The tool
will warn you if:

- Any proposed entity still has an unconfirmed identifier.
- Any accepted mention has annotator disagreements that you have not reviewed.

Both are soft warnings — you can proceed anyway. After confirming, the article is
marked as curated and you are returned to the curation queue.
